      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Spreading the Web</strong><br>\nTo ensure widespread adoption of the web, Berners-Lee decided not to patent his invention. Instead, he made the software available on a royalty-free basis, believing it should be a free and open platform for all to use. His decision to forgo personal profit from his invention was crucial in allowing the web to expand rapidly around the globe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Foundation of the World Wide Web Consortium</strong><br>\nIn 1994, Berners-Lee founded the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. It comprised various companies willing to create standards and recommendations to improve the quality of the web. Having a central organization like W3C helped ensure that the web remained open and compatible across different technologies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Awards</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Tim Berners-Lee has been honored with numerous awards. He was knighted by Queen Elizabeth II in 2004 and has received the Turing Award, often called the 'Nobel Prize of Computing.' His contributions have been acknowledged worldwide for their impact on information sharing and communication.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
