          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Current Work and Vision</strong><br>\nBerners-Lee continues to work on advancing the web through his work with W3C and as a professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and the University of Oxford. He advocates for data rights and privacy, ensuring that the web serves humanity in the best way possible. He has been a vocal proponent of net neutrality and responsible tech development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Views on the Web's Future</strong><br>\nBerners-Lee has expressed concern over issues such as disinformation, privacy breaches, and the web's misuse. He advocates for a 'Magna Carta' for the web, calling for rights and freedoms for all online users. He stresses the importance of fighting for the web's openness and accessibility to prevent it from being controlled by a few entities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Tim Berners-Lee is immense. As the inventor of the World Wide Web, he revolutionized how people access and share information, and his work has influenced countless aspects of everyday life. His commitment to an open web has earned him a lasting place in history as one of the most significant innovators of our time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
